    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a8699ac3-b682-532f-89bc-ae6b551f66c9",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y'+12 xy=0$ for $y\\ne0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y'+12 xy=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12 xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 12 xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 12 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 12 x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (- 12 x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\ln|y| & =- 6 x^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln|y|=- 6 x^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln|y| & =- 6 x^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & =e^{- 6 x^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & =e^{- 6 x^{2}}\\cdot e^C \\\\ |y| & =K e^{- 6 x^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=e^C$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K e^{- 6 x^{2}}.\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
