    "question": {
      ":question/id": "851a7300-de84-5a7b-8e19-491cd5f0c760",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y'-xy=0$ for $y\\ne0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y'-xy=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\ln|y| & =\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln|y|=\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln|y| & =\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ |y| & =e^{\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & =e^{\\frac{x^{2}}{2}}\\cdot e^C \\\\ |y| & =K e^{\\frac{x^{2}}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=e^C$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K e^{\\frac{x^{2}}{2}}.\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "92db21e2-b03f-5be0-bf10-e6f9c027d717",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "a879415b-e974-542a-a285-1fe9bfb6524d",
